# XSH random(), srandom(), initstate() and setstate()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# All four are XSI. The generator's values are not specified; the tests use
# only range, repeatability, equal sequences where the page says two set-ups
# are the same, and the pointers initstate() and setstate() return. State
# arrays are suitably aligned for long.

assertion  XSH.random.001
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/random.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       random() values are in the range 0 to 2^31-1 and not constant over
           1000 calls; srandom(s) again with the same seed s repeats the
           same 100 values, for several seeds.
test       t/001-repeat-range.c

assertion  XSH.random.002
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       random() before any seeding gives the sequence that srandom(1)
           produces: 50 values taken first equal 50 taken after srandom(1).
test       t/002-default-seed.c

assertion  XSH.random.003
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       With no earlier initstate() call random() behaves as though
           initstate(1, state, 128) had been called: the first 50 values
           equal the 50 after initstate(1, state, 128).
test       t/003-default-state.c

assertion  XSH.random.004
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/random.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       initstate() and setstate() return a pointer to the previous state
           array: the first initstate(.., A, ..) returns non-null; a second
           initstate(.., B, ..) returns A; setstate(A) then returns B and
           setstate(B) returns A.
test       t/004-previous-state.c

assertion  XSH.random.005
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       setstate() switches between state arrays without losing their
           progress: with two initialised arrays A and B, five values from A,
           five from B and five more from A give the same values as five
           from A then five further from A, and B's five equal a fresh
           run of B.
test       t/005-switch-state.c

assertion  XSH.random.006
anchor     functions/random.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       initstate() with a size less than 8 returns a null pointer.
test       t/006-small-size.c

assertion  XSH.random.007
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       Sizes that are not one of 8, 32, 64, 128 and 256 are rounded down
           to the nearest of them (values of 8 up to 31 giving the 8-byte
           generator): with the same seed the sequences for 20, 31, 33, 100,
           127, 255 and 300 bytes equal those for 8, 8, 32, 64, 64, 128 and
           256; and every size gives values in 0 to 2^31-1.
test       t/007-size-rounding.c

assertion  XSH.random.008
anchor     functions/random.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/random.html#APPLICATION USAGE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       For n of 8, 32 and 128, calling initstate(s, A, n) twice with the
           same seed gives the same values both times, and calling
           srandom(t) twice on that array gives the same values both times.
note       The page does not say that initstate(s, ...) and srandom(s) give
           the same sequence, so the two are compared only with themselves.
test       t/008-restart.c
